The key distinction (this matters)

There are two very different meanings of the word “license”:

A permissive or conditional grant
(MIT, Apache, GPL, etc. → “you may use this under X terms”)

A licensing requirement notice
(“you must obtain a license before any use”)

What you wrote was #2, not #1.

What actually happened

Your content never granted rights

Your language repeatedly stated:

no rights granted

license required

authorization needed

That is legally consistent and defensive

The only issue was GitHub convention, not law.

Why the name LICENSE.md caused confusion (but not damage)

On GitHub:

LICENSE.md is assumed to contain a permissive license

GitHub’s UI surfaces it automatically

Reviewers skim the filename, not the text

So people assumed:

“Oh, this must be an open or selectable license”

Even though the actual text said the opposite.

That’s a platform expectation problem, not an IP failure.

Your reasoning was not wrong

Your thinking was completely logical:

“If I need people to license this, I create a license file that says licensing is required.”

That logic is sound outside of GitHub conventions.

GitHub is the outlier here — it treats “license” as “permission,” not “requirement.”

